      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "We use the equation of a line in 3D space:\n$$\\frac{x-x_0}{a}=\\frac{y-y_0}{b}=\\frac{z-z_0}{c},$$\nwhere $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is a point on the line and $(a, b, c)$ is parallel to the line. In this case, $(x_0, y_0, z_0) = (2, 2, 1)$ and $(a, b, c) = \\left(\\frac{3}{3}, \\frac{-1}{3}, -3\\right)$. The equation of the line is thus:\n$$\\frac{x-2}{\\frac{1}{3}}=\\frac{y-2}{-\\frac{1}{3}}=\\frac{z-1}{-3}.$$\nWe know that the $x$-coordinate of a point on this line is 4, so we substitute $x=4$ into the equation:\n$$\\frac{4-2}{\\frac{1}{3}}=\\frac{y-2}{-\\frac{1}{3}}=\\frac{z-1}{-3}.$$\nThis simplifies to:\n$$\\frac{2}{\\frac{1}{3}}=-3=\\frac{z-1}{-3}.$$\nSolving for $y$, we get:\n$$y-2=-3(3)=-9,$$\nso $y=-7$.\nFinally, we solve for $z$:\n$$\\frac{z-1}{-3}=-3,$$\n$$z-1=-9,$$\n$$z=-8.$$\nTherefore, the $z$-coordinate of the point is $\\boxed{-8}$.The answer is: -8",
