      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point on the line that passes through the points \\((2,2,1)\\) and \\((5,1,-2)\\) with an \\( x \\)-coordinate of 4, we start by determining the parametric equations of the line.\n\nThe direction vector of the line can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the two points:\n\\[\n\\text{Direction vector} = (5-2, 1-2, -2-1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\nThe parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n\\[\nx = 2 + 3t, \\quad y = 2 - t, \\quad z = 1 - 3t\n\\]\nWe need to find the value of \\( t \\) when \\( x = 4 \\). Substituting \\( x = 4 \\) into the equation \\( x = 2 + 3t \\), we get:\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\nNow, we substitute \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\) into the equation for \\( z \\):\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3 \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\nThus, the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}\n\\]",
