      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point on the line that passes through the points \\((2,2,1)\\) and \\((5,1,-2)\\) and has an \\( x \\)-coordinate of 4, we first need to determine the parametric equations of the line. The parametric form of a line can be written as:\n\n\\[\n(x, y, z) = (x_0, y_0, z_0) + t(d_x, d_y, d_z)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((x_0, y_0, z_0)\\) is a point on the line and \\((d_x, d_y, d_z)\\) is the direction vector of the line. The direction vector can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the two given points:\n\n\\[\n(d_x, d_y, d_z) = (5-2, 1-2, -2-1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\nNow, we can write the parametric equations of the line using the point \\((2,2,1)\\) as \\((x_0, y_0, z_0)\\):\n\n\\[\n(x, y, z) = (2, 2, 1) + t(3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the following parametric equations:\n\n\\[\nx = 2 + 3t\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2 - t\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3t\n\\]\n\nWe know that the \\( x \\)-coordinate of the point on the line is 4. Therefore, we set \\( x = 4 \\) and solve for \\( t \\):\n\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t\n\\]\n\\[\n4 - 2 = 3t\n\\]\n\\[\n2 = 3t\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\), we substitute this value into the equation for \\( z \\) to find the \\( z \\)-coordinate:\n\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - 2\n\\]\n\\[\nz = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
